"""mojo_aws_client: an AWS client generated from a botocore service model at build time.

    load("@komira//third_party/botocore:models.bzl", "botocore_model")

    mojo_aws_client(
        name = "komira_aws_logs",
        model = botocore_model("logs").model,
        model_sha256 = botocore_model("logs").sha256,
        operations = ["GetLogEvents"],
        mode = "pure",                  # or "client"
        customizations = [],            # optional; "s3" or "route53", each for its own service
        # optional; set both or neither
        endpoint_rules = botocore_model("logs").endpoint_rules,
        partitions = botocore_model("logs").partitions,
        deps = [
            "komira//src/komira_aws_core:komira_aws_core",
            "komira//src/komira_json:komira_json",
        ],
    )

is two targets:

  * `<name>_gen`, the generation action: komira//tools/build/proto-codegen:
    aws-client-gen writes `gen/<name>/` with `__init__.mojo`, the module
    `<name>.mojo` and `_layout_probe.mojo` (one `size_of` per emitted
    struct), plus a copy of each `hand_srcs` file. Nothing is compiled
    there.
  * `<name>`, an ordinary `mojo_library` over those files, so the client is
    welded exactly like a hand-written library: its `test_srcs` gate its
    `.mojoc`. The layout probe is its first test (a generated `test_srcs`
    entry), then the environment scan (below), then the caller's.
    `<name>[gen]` is the generated directory (the `gen` attr of
    mojo_library); `<name>[gen][<file>]` one file of it. The client is
    imported as `<name>.<name>`.

Environment scan. `<name>_gen[_no_env_reads.mojo]` is a test written at
analysis for this client, welded as the library's second `test_srcs` entry,
with every file of the package staged as its data at `gen/<file>`. It
fails if any of them names one of the reads the scan knows (`getenv`,
`_read_env`, `std.os`, `expanduser`, `expandvars`, komira_aws_core's
`EnvSource`, `ProcessEnv` and `aws_endpoint_config`), reaches the FFI a
read goes through (`komira_libc`, `external_call`, `DLHandle`), or, with
`endpoint_rules`, takes komira_aws_core's ruleset-free `resolve_endpoint(`
path around the ruleset. It also fails if a file imports a module outside
an allow-list: the package itself, the runtime the generator imports
(komira_aws_core, komira_http_client, komira_http_core, komira_json,
komira_retry, komira_xml) and std.sys, each with its submodules. The scan
reads an import statement (`from X ...`, `import A, B as c`) where a
statement can start: a line, at any indent, or after a `;` or a `:` (a
one-line function body), in the file with its comments and string literals
blanked out and each backslash-ended line joined to the next. A backtick
identifier is read whole (a quote or `#` in it is part of the name), and a
backslash pairs with the byte after it in every string, raw ones included
(Mojo refuses `r"\\"` as unterminated). A file with a t-string is refused:
its braces hold code with strings of their own, which the scan does not
lex. So is a file with an ASCII control byte other than a tab or a line
feed: Mojo ends a line at a lone carriage return, a vertical tab and a form
feed, and the scan reads only line feeds. Mojo refuses an import inside a
block such as an `if`.
Every variable is refused, the provider-standard `AWS_*` ones included: a
client takes each input as a parameter, and reading the credential
variables is komira_aws_core's EnvSource. The scan also checks that it read
this target's whole client: the module is in the list it scans, its header
names this target's model sha256, its operations and its mode, and it ends
with the line aws-client-gen writes last (`# End of <name>, generated by
aws-client-gen.`). A caller's test of the same file name is refused (two
`test_srcs` share it).

Model. `model` is one botocore `service-2.json`, normally
`botocore_model("<service>").model` of //third_party/botocore, and
`model_sha256` its digest (`botocore_model("<service>").sha256`); the
generator refuses a model whose bytes have another digest. The botocore
service id is read from the model's path (`<service>/<api version>/
service-2.json`, botocore's data layout) unless `service` names it.

Scope. `operations` is required and non-empty, checked at analysis: the
generator is operation-scoped and emits only the closure of the operations
named. Each entry is one botocore operation name (`GetLogEvents`); an entry
holding `,` (the generator's list separator), whitespace or anything else is
refused here rather than mis-split. An operation the model does not declare
is refused by the generator, in the build.

Mode. `pure` emits the shapes and `build_<op>_request` / `parse_<op>_response`
with no transport (`--pure-only`); `client` adds the signed-send surface,
which imports the komira_aws_core transport names, komira_http_core,
komira_http_client (the constructor's `HttpClientConfig`) and komira_retry
(the seams of the send over injected seams).

Endpoints. `endpoint_rules` is the service's botocore endpoint ruleset
(`endpoint-rule-set-1.json`, normally `botocore_model("<service>")
.endpoint_rules`) and `partitions` the partition table it reads
(`botocore_model("<service>").partitions`); the two are set together or not
at all. With them the module embeds both and resolves each operation's
endpoint through `komira_aws_core.EndpointRuleSet`: a `<Prefix>EndpointConfig`
holding the ruleset's built-in and client context parameters, and a
`resolve_<op>_endpoint` binding the operation's context parameters. The
generator refuses a binding the ruleset does not declare. Without them, the
module's header lists the endpoint bindings of the model it does not apply.

Overrides. `overrides` is the generator's hand-override manifest (a JSON
file naming, per operation, the hand-written owner of its plain verb), and
`hand_srcs` the hand-written `.mojo` modules that own them: the generator
checks each named owner exists in them, and they are copied into the
package next to the generated module. Each needs the other.

Customizations. `customizations` is a closed set, checked here: `s3` applies
botocore's S3 handling the model does not state (a 200 whose body is an
<Error> is an error; an `Expires` header that is not a date is left unset;
with `endpoint_rules`, a request path's leading `/{Bucket}` is dropped,
because the ruleset puts the bucket in the URL), and the generator refuses
it unless the model's serviceId is `S3` and its protocol is restXml.
`route53` applies botocore's Route 53 Id handling (each top-level input
member of shape ResourceId, DelegationSetId or ChangeId is sent as the part
after its last `/`, so an Id Route 53 answered with can be passed back), and
the generator refuses it unless the model's serviceId is `Route 53` and its
protocol is restXml.

Runtime. `deps` is required and non-empty, and nothing is added to it: the
generated code imports its runtime (komira_aws_core; komira_json for a JSON
protocol, komira_xml for restXml, awsQuery and ec2Query; and in client mode
komira_http_core, komira_http_client and komira_retry), which the caller
names as `komira//` labels, or as stubs in a test. They are the library's
`deps`, so they take what `mojo_library.deps` takes; `<name>_gen` sees only
their count.

Every refusal of the rule happens at analysis, in `<name>_gen`, so a BUCK
file with one wrong mojo_aws_client still loads.
"""

load("@komira//tools/build/lint:doc_tree.bzl", "declares_docs")
load("@komira//tools/build/mojo:defs.bzl", "mojo_library")

# The generated layout probe's file name (as emit.rs's LAYOUT_PROBE_FILE for
# the proto plugin), and the generator's list separator for --operations
# (main_aws_client.rs splits on it).
_LAYOUT_PROBE = "_layout_probe.mojo"
_LIST_SEPARATOR = ","

# The environment scan written for each client (see the module docstring):
# its file name, and where it is declared, which is its test_data key.
_ENV_SCAN = "_no_env_reads.mojo"
_ENV_SCAN_PATH = "tests/" + _ENV_SCAN

# What the scan refuses in every file of the package, and, with a ruleset,
# what it refuses beside them (komira_aws_core's endpoint path for a client
# with no ruleset, whose override a caller takes from the environment
# through aws_endpoint_config).
_ENV_BANNED = [
    "getenv",
    "_read_env",
    "std.os",
    "EnvSource",
    "ProcessEnv",
    "aws_endpoint_config",
    "komira_libc",
    "external_call",
    "DLHandle",
    "expanduser",
    "expandvars",
]
_ENV_BANNED_WITH_RULES = ["resolve_endpoint("]

# The modules a file of the package may import, beside its own package (by
# name, or relatively): the runtime the generator imports, and std.sys for
# the layout probe's size_of. A module counts with its submodules. Every
# other import is refused, so a read through a module the banned names do
# not spell (std.pathlib's Path.home, `from std import os`) is refused too.
_ENV_IMPORTS = [
    "komira_aws_core",
    "komira_http_client",
    "komira_http_core",
    "komira_json",
    "komira_retry",
    "komira_xml",
    "std.sys",
]

# The line aws-client-gen writes last in the module (emit_aws's `emit`), for
# the module `{}`: the scan requires it once, at the end, so it read the
# whole module.
_MODULE_END = "# End of {}, generated by aws-client-gen."

_ENV_SCAN_TEMPLATE = """# Generated by mojo_aws_client (tools/build/cloud/aws.bzl) for
# {label}: the package reads no environment. Each of its files is staged as this
# test's data at gen/<file>; the test reads each one and fails if any names
# a way to read the environment, reaches the FFI a read would go through,
# takes komira_aws_core's ruleset-free endpoint path around the service's
# ruleset, or imports a module outside its allow-list. komira_aws_core's
# test_env_source_only holds the same line for the core, where the one read
# site is its EnvSource.
from std.testing import assert_equal, assert_true


comptime _DIR = "gen/"
comptime _MODULE = "{module}"
comptime _MODULE_END = "{module_end}"


def _files() -> List[String]:
    return [
{files}    ]


def _count(hay: String, needle: String) -> Int:
    var n = 0
    var at = hay.find(needle)
    while at >= 0:
        n += 1
        at = hay.find(needle, at + needle.byte_length())
    return n


def _read(name: String) raises -> String:
    with open(String(_DIR) + name, "r") as f:
        return f.read()


def _name_byte(c: Int) -> Bool:
    return c == 95 or (c >= 48 and c <= 57) or (c >= 65 and c <= 90) or (c >= 97 and c <= 122)


def _control_byte(name: String, text: String) raises:
    \"\"\"Raises, naming the byte, if `text` has an ASCII control byte (below
    0x20) other than a tab or a line feed. Mojo ends a line at a lone carriage
    return, at a vertical tab and at a form feed, and joins a backslash before
    a carriage return and line feed; _code reads only line feeds, so an import
    after one of these would not be read. The other control bytes are refused
    too (fail closed), whatever Mojo makes of them.\"\"\"
    var b = text.as_bytes()
    for i in range(len(b)):
        var c = Int(b[i])
        if c < 32 and c != 9 and c != 10:
            var digits = String("0123456789ABCDEF")
            var what = String()
            if c == 11:
                what = " (vertical tab)"
            elif c == 12:
                what = " (form feed)"
            elif c == 13:
                what = " (carriage return)"
            raise Error(
                name + " has the control byte 0x" + String(digits[byte=c // 16:c // 16 + 1])
                + String(digits[byte=c % 16:c % 16 + 1]) + what
                + ", which the environment scan does not read"
            )


def _code(name: String, text: String) raises -> String:
    \"\"\"`text` with each comment and each string literal replaced by one space,
    a backslash that ends a line replaced by one space with that newline (Mojo
    joins the two lines), and other newlines kept. A backtick identifier is
    kept whole: a quote or a `#` in it is part of the name. In a string, raw
    (prefix `r`) or not, a backslash and the byte after it are one pair, so
    an escaped quote does not end it (Mojo refuses `r"\\\\"` as unterminated).
    Raises on a t-string (prefix `t`, `rt` or `tr`, any case): its braces hold
    code with string literals of their own, which this does not lex. Raises
    on an ASCII control byte other than a tab or a line feed (_control_byte).\"\"\"
    _control_byte(name, text)
    var b = text.as_bytes()
    var n = len(b)
    var out = String()
    var start = 0
    var i = 0
    while i < n:
        var c = Int(b[i])
        if c == 35:  # `#`: a comment, to the end of its line
            out += String(text[byte=start:i])
            while i < n and Int(b[i]) != 10:
                i += 1
            start = i
        elif c == 96:  # a backtick identifier, to its closing backtick
            i += 1
            while i < n and Int(b[i]) != 96 and Int(b[i]) != 10:
                i += 1
            if i < n and Int(b[i]) == 96:
                i += 1
        elif c == 92 and i + 1 < n and Int(b[i + 1]) == 10:  # a line joined to the next
            out += String(text[byte=start:i]) + " "
            i += 2
            start = i
        elif c == 34 or c == 39:  # a double or single quote: a string literal
            var p = i
            while p > 0 and _name_byte(Int(b[p - 1])):
                p -= 1
            var prefix = String(text[byte=p:i]).lower()
            if prefix == "t" or prefix == "rt" or prefix == "tr":
                raise Error(
                    name + " has a t-string, which the environment scan does"
                    " not read"
                )
            out += String(text[byte=start:i]) + " "
            var triple = i + 2 < n and Int(b[i + 1]) == c and Int(b[i + 2]) == c
            i += 3 if triple else 1
            while i < n:
                var d = Int(b[i])
                if d == 92:  # a backslash: it and the next byte are one pair
                    i += 2
                elif triple and d == c and i + 2 < n and Int(b[i + 1]) == c and Int(b[i + 2]) == c:
                    i += 3
                    break
                elif not triple and d == c:
                    i += 1
                    break
                elif not triple and d == 10:
                    break
                else:
                    i += 1
            i = min(i, n)
            start = i
        else:
            i += 1
    out += String(text[byte=start:n])
    return out^


def _imports(name: String, text: String) raises -> List[String]:
    \"\"\"The module each import statement of `text` names: the `X` of `from X ...`
    and each `A` of `import A, B as c`. A statement is read where it starts:
    a line, at any indent, or after a `;` or a `:`, in `text` without its
    comments and string literals (_code).\"\"\"
    var out = List[String]()
    var code = _code(name, text).replace("\\t", " ").replace(";", "\\n").replace(":", "\\n")
    for raw in code.split("\\n"):
        var line = String(String(raw).strip())
        if line.startswith("from "):
            var rest = String(String(line[byte=5:]).strip())
            var sp = rest.find(" ")
            out.append(String(rest[byte=0:sp]) if sp >= 0 else rest)
        elif line.startswith("import "):
            for item in String(line[byte=7:]).split(","):
                var it = String(String(item).strip())
                var sp = it.find(" ")
                out.append(String(it[byte=0:sp]) if sp >= 0 else it)
    return out^


def _allowed(module: String) -> Bool:
    if module.startswith("."):
        return True
    var allowed: List[String] = [
{imports}    ]
    for i in range(len(allowed)):
        if module == allowed[i] or module.startswith(allowed[i] + "."):
            return True
    return False


def test_no_environment_read() raises:
    var banned: List[String] = [
{banned}    ]
    var files = _files()
    for i in range(len(files)):
        var text = _read(files[i])
        for j in range(len(banned)):
            assert_equal(
                _count(text, banned[j]),
                0,
                files[i] + " names " + banned[j] + "; a mojo_aws_client"
                " package takes every input as a parameter",
            )
        var modules = _imports(files[i], text)
        for j in range(len(modules)):
            assert_true(
                _allowed(modules[j]),
                files[i] + " imports " + modules[j] + ", which is not on the"
                " environment scan's import allow-list (mojo_aws_client's"
                " _ENV_IMPORTS)",
            )


def test_the_scan_saw_the_client() raises:
    # Not vacuous: the module is in the list test_no_environment_read reads,
    # it is the one generated for this target (from its model, for its
    # operations, in its mode), and it is whole (it ends with the line the
    # generator writes last).
    var files = _files()
    var at = -1
    for i in range(len(files)):
        if files[i] == _MODULE:
            at = i
    assert_true(at >= 0, "the scan's file list does not hold " + String(_MODULE))
    var text = _read(files[at])
    assert_equal(_count(text, "\\n#   model sha256 : {sha256}\\n"), 1, "the model sha256")
    assert_equal(_count(text, "\\n#   operations   : {operations}\\n"), 1, "the operations")
    assert_equal(_count(text, "\\n#   mode         : {mode}\\n"), 1, "the mode")
    assert_equal(_count(text, String(_MODULE_END)), 1, "the module's last line, once")
    assert_true(
        text.endswith("\\n" + String(_MODULE_END) + "\\n"),
        String(_MODULE) + " does not end with the line aws-client-gen writes last",
    )


def main() raises:
    test_no_environment_read()
    test_the_scan_saw_the_client()
    print("OK")
"""

def _mojo_str(s):
    """`s` as a Mojo string literal's contents."""
    return s.replace("\\", "\\\\").replace("\"", "\\\"").replace("\n", "\\n")

def _env_scan_text(ctx, files):
    banned = _ENV_BANNED + (_ENV_BANNED_WITH_RULES if ctx.attrs.endpoint_rules else [])
    return _ENV_SCAN_TEMPLATE.format(
        label = "{}//{}:{}".format(ctx.label.cell, ctx.label.package, ctx.attrs.import_name),
        banned = "".join(['        "{}",\n'.format(_mojo_str(b)) for b in banned]),
        files = "".join(['        "{}",\n'.format(_mojo_str(f)) for f in files]),
        imports = "".join(['        "{}",\n'.format(_mojo_str(m)) for m in _ENV_IMPORTS + [ctx.attrs.import_name]]),
        module = _mojo_str(ctx.attrs.import_name + ".mojo"),
        module_end = _mojo_str(_MODULE_END.format(ctx.attrs.import_name)),
        sha256 = _mojo_str(ctx.attrs.model_sha256),
        # The header lists them as the generator orders them: by name.
        operations = _mojo_str(", ".join(sorted(ctx.attrs.operations))),
        mode = "pure (no transport)" if ctx.attrs.mode == "pure" else "client",
    )

_GEN_SCRIPT = """
# $1 busybox, $2 generator, $3 output package directory, $4 module name;
# $5 number of hand sources, then those; then the generator's arguments.
BB="$1"; GEN="$2"; OUT="$3"; MOD="$4"; N="$5"; shift 5
case "$BB" in /*) ;; *) BB="$PWD/$BB" ;; esac
case "$GEN" in /*) ;; *) GEN="$PWD/$GEN" ;; esac
"$BB" mkdir -p "$OUT"
HAND=""
while [ "$N" -gt 0 ]; do
    HAND="$HAND $1"
    shift; N=$((N - 1))
done
"$GEN" --module "$MOD" --out "$OUT/$MOD.mojo" --probe-out "$OUT/_layout_probe.mojo" "$@"
printf '%s\\n' '# Generated by mojo_aws_client (tools/build/cloud/aws.bzl): the package of the' \\
    '# module generated from this target'"'"'s botocore model. Do not edit.' > "$OUT/__init__.mojo"
n=3
for h in $HAND; do
    "$BB" cp "$h" "$OUT/"
    n=$((n + 1))
done
# Exactly the expected files, none empty: an empty module is a silent
# generator failure, and an unexpected one would not be compiled.
for f in "$MOD.mojo" _layout_probe.mojo; do
    if [ ! -s "$OUT/$f" ]; then
        echo "aws-client-gen wrote no code (or an empty file) for the expected $f" >&2
        exit 1
    fi
done
if [ "$("$BB" ls -A "$OUT" | "$BB" wc -l)" != "$n" ]; then
    echo "mojo_aws_client: the generated package holds files that were not declared:" >&2
    "$BB" ls -A "$OUT" >&2
    exit 1
fi
"""

def _basename(path):
    return path.rsplit("/", 1)[-1]

def _service_of(ctx):
    if ctx.attrs.service:
        if not regex_match("^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]*$", ctx.attrs.service):
            fail("{}: `service` `{}` is not a botocore service id".format(ctx.label, ctx.attrs.service))
        return ctx.attrs.service
    parts = ctx.attrs.model.short_path.split("/")
    if len(parts) < 3 or parts[-1] != "service-2.json" or not regex_match("^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]*$", parts[-3]):
        fail("{}: the botocore service id cannot be read from the model path `{}` (botocore's layout is `<service>/<api version>/service-2.json`); set `service`".format(ctx.label, ctx.attrs.model.short_path))
    return parts[-3]

def _aws_client_gen_impl(ctx):
    import_name = ctx.attrs.import_name
    if not regex_match("^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*$", import_name):
        fail("{}: import name `{}` is not a Mojo identifier".format(ctx.label, import_name))
    if not ctx.attrs.operations:
        fail("{}: `operations` is empty. A mojo_aws_client generates the operations it names, never a whole service: list the botocore operations the client calls".format(ctx.label))
    seen = {}
    for op in ctx.attrs.operations:
        if not regex_match("^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9]*$", op):
            fail("{}: `operations` entry `{}` is not a botocore operation name (`GetLogEvents`); entries are separate list items, never joined with `{}`".format(ctx.label, op, _LIST_SEPARATOR))
        if op in seen:
            fail("{}: `operations` names `{}` twice".format(ctx.label, op))
        seen[op] = True
    if ctx.attrs.runtime_dep_count == 0:
        fail("{}: `deps` is empty. The generated code imports its runtime (komira_aws_core; komira_json for a JSON protocol, komira_xml for restXml, awsQuery and ec2Query; and in client mode komira_http_core, komira_http_client and komira_retry); name it, as komira// labels. No runtime is added by default.".format(ctx.label))
    if ctx.attrs.overrides and not ctx.attrs.hand_srcs:
        fail("{}: `overrides` is set and `hand_srcs` is empty: the manifest names hand-written owners, and they are its `hand_srcs`".format(ctx.label))
    if ctx.attrs.hand_srcs and not ctx.attrs.overrides:
        fail("{}: `hand_srcs` is set and `overrides` is not: hand-written modules of a mojo_aws_client own the operations its overrides manifest names".format(ctx.label))
    if (ctx.attrs.endpoint_rules == None) != (ctx.attrs.partitions == None):
        fail("{}: `endpoint_rules` and `partitions` are set together: the ruleset's aws.partition reads the partition table".format(ctx.label))
    if _ENV_SCAN_PATH in ctx.attrs.test_data_keys:
        fail("{}: `test_data` has an entry for `{}`, the generated environment scan; its data is the package, staged by the rule".format(ctx.label, _ENV_SCAN_PATH))
    reserved = {"__init__.mojo": True, _LAYOUT_PROBE: True, import_name + ".mojo": True}
    for p in ctx.attrs.hand_src_paths:
        if ":" in p or not p.endswith(".mojo"):
            fail("{}: `hand_srcs` entry `{}` is not a source path of a `.mojo` file. `hand_srcs` takes source paths only, never a label: the library's file names are derived from these paths".format(ctx.label, p))
        b = _basename(p)
        if b in reserved:
            fail("{}: `hand_srcs` entry `{}` has the name of a generated or another hand-written file, `{}`".format(ctx.label, p, b))
        reserved[b] = True

    gen_args = [
        "--model",
        ctx.attrs.model,
        "--model-sha256",
        ctx.attrs.model_sha256,
        "--service",
        _service_of(ctx),
        "--operations",
        _LIST_SEPARATOR.join(ctx.attrs.operations),
        "--probe-import",
        import_name + "." + import_name,
    ]
    if ctx.attrs.mode == "pure":
        gen_args.append("--pure-only")
    for c in ctx.attrs.customizations:
        gen_args += ["--customization", c]
    if ctx.attrs.endpoint_rules:
        gen_args += ["--endpoint-rules", ctx.attrs.endpoint_rules, "--partitions", ctx.attrs.partitions]
    if ctx.attrs.overrides:
        gen_args += ["--overrides", ctx.attrs.overrides]
        for h in ctx.attrs.hand_srcs:
            gen_args += ["--hand-src", h]

    bb = ctx.attrs._busybox[DefaultInfo].default_outputs[0]
    gen = ctx.attrs._gen[DefaultInfo].default_outputs[0]

    # One directory output: every file of a package must share one
    # directory, and separately declared outputs need not.
    gen_dir = ctx.actions.declare_output("gen/" + import_name, dir = True)
    ctx.actions.run(
        cmd_args(
            bb,
            "sh",
            "-euc",
            _GEN_SCRIPT,
            "sh",
            bb,
            gen,
            gen_dir.as_output(),
            import_name,
            str(len(ctx.attrs.hand_srcs)),
            ctx.attrs.hand_srcs,
            gen_args,
        ),
        category = "aws_client_gen",
    )

    files = ["__init__.mojo", import_name + ".mojo", _LAYOUT_PROBE] + [h.basename for h in ctx.attrs.hand_srcs]
    scan = ctx.actions.write(_ENV_SCAN_PATH, _env_scan_text(ctx, files))
    return [DefaultInfo(
        default_output = gen_dir,
        sub_targets = {f: [DefaultInfo(default_output = gen_dir.project(f))] for f in files} | {
            _ENV_SCAN: [DefaultInfo(default_output = scan)],
        },
    )]

_aws_client_gen = rule(
    impl = _aws_client_gen_impl,
    attrs = {
        "customizations": attrs.list(attrs.enum(["route53", "s3"]), default = []),
        "endpoint_rules": attrs.option(attrs.source(), default = None),
        "hand_src_paths": attrs.list(attrs.string(), default = []),
        "hand_srcs": attrs.list(attrs.source(), default = []),
        "import_name": attrs.string(),
        "mode": attrs.enum(["pure", "client"]),
        "model": attrs.source(),
        "model_sha256": attrs.string(),
        "operations": attrs.list(attrs.string()),
        "overrides": attrs.option(attrs.source(), default = None),
        "partitions": attrs.option(attrs.source(), default = None),
        # `len(deps)` of the library, so an empty runtime is refused at
        # analysis. A count, not the labels: the generator has no edge to the
        # runtime, and `deps` keeps every kind `mojo_library.deps` accepts.
        "runtime_dep_count": attrs.int(),
        "service": attrs.option(attrs.string(), default = None),
        # The keys of the library's caller-given `test_data`, so that an entry
        # for the environment scan is refused at analysis.
        "test_data_keys": attrs.list(attrs.string(), default = []),
        "_busybox": attrs.exec_dep(default = "komira//tools/build/toolchains:busybox"),
        "_gen": attrs.exec_dep(default = "komira//tools/build/proto-codegen:aws-client-gen"),
    },
)

def _aws_client(
        name,
        model,
        model_sha256,
        operations,
        deps,
        mode = "pure",
        service = None,
        customizations = [],
        endpoint_rules = None,
        partitions = None,
        overrides = None,
        hand_srcs = [],
        test_srcs = [],
        test_data = {},
        visibility = None,
        **kwargs):
    """See the module docstring. `kwargs` go to the mojo_library (test_env, readme)."""
    gen = name + "_gen"
    vis = {"visibility": visibility} if visibility != None else {}
    _aws_client_gen(
        name = gen,
        customizations = customizations,
        endpoint_rules = endpoint_rules,
        partitions = partitions,
        hand_src_paths = hand_srcs,
        hand_srcs = hand_srcs,
        import_name = name,
        mode = mode,
        model = model,
        model_sha256 = model_sha256,
        operations = operations,
        overrides = overrides,
        runtime_dep_count = len(deps),
        service = service,
        test_data_keys = list(test_data.keys()),
        **vis
    )

    # The file names the generation action is held to (it refuses a missing,
    # empty or extra file), so they can be named here. A hand_srcs entry that
    # is not a `.mojo` path is left out rather than turned into a sub-target
    # name: `<name>_gen` refuses it, and that refusal is what the build
    # reports.
    hand = [_basename(h) for h in hand_srcs if h.endswith(".mojo") and ":" not in h]
    srcs = [":{}[{}]".format(gen, f) for f in ["__init__.mojo", name + ".mojo"] + hand]
    scan_data = {
        "gen/" + f: ":{}[{}]".format(gen, f)
        for f in ["__init__.mojo", _LAYOUT_PROBE, name + ".mojo"] + hand
    }
    mojo_library(
        name = name,
        srcs = srcs,
        deps = deps,
        gen = ":" + gen,
        test_data = test_data | {_ENV_SCAN_PATH: scan_data},
        test_srcs = [
            ":{}[{}]".format(gen, _LAYOUT_PROBE),
            ":{}[{}]".format(gen, _ENV_SCAN),
        ] + test_srcs,
        **(vis | kwargs)
    )

mojo_aws_client = declares_docs(_aws_client)
